                <pb facs="0671" n="581" sameAs="frus:frus1875v01/pg581"/>

                <head>No. 255.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">General Schenck to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1874-11-03"><hi rendition="#italic">November</hi> 3, 1874</date>.
                        (Received November 19.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 633.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to ray No. 629, and my
                    correspondence with Lord Derby in relation to the request in behalf of Admiral
                    Davis and the astronomers of the United States engaged in observing the transit
                    of Venus for a free use of telegraph-wires in certain of the British colonies, I
                    have now to transmit to you a copy of another note on the subject from his
                    lordship, received yesterday.</p>
                <p>In this note I am informed that the secretary of state for the colonies, (Lord
                    Caernarvon,) considering that it is too late to communicate by writing, has
                    addressed a telegram to the governments of Victoria and South Australia,
                    expressing the hope of Her Majesty’s government that, on the application of the
                    gentlemen employed in the work under the authority of our Navy Department, the
                    facilities requested will be granted them by the colonial authorities.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>ROBT. C. SCHENCK.</signed></closer>



                <xi:include href="d259-a1.xml" xpointer="d259-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
